<Docs
  path="/docs/providers"
  heading="Providers"
  lead="KalCode works with the AI coding tools you already use, on your own accounts. Connecting them is free on every plan."
>
  <h2>Supported providers</h2>
  <p>KalCode runs threads with these providers today:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claude Code</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with your own Claude account.</li>
    <li><strong>Codex</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with your own personal ChatGPT plan, with its documented approval and sandbox settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Gemini CLI</strong> — the local CLI, signed in with your own Google account, with its documented non-interactive output modes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    More providers can be added later through a documented adapter contract. Each integration is
    verified against the provider's current official documentation before it ships.
  </p>

  <h2>How connections work</h2>
  <p>
    KalCode uses only legitimate, documented integration methods: each provider's own local CLI,
    signed in through that provider's official sign-in flow. It never scrapes
    browser credentials, copies hidden session credentials, or reverse-engineers private
    authentication.
  </p>

  <h2>Signing in</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in to each provider inside KalCode: open Providers, then Accounts, add an account and choose
    Sign in. KalCode runs that provider's own official sign-in for that account only. Claude Code and
    Codex open their browser sign-in, and Gemini CLI opens Google sign-in in your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each account has its own managed provider profile. Signing a CLI in from a separate terminal signs
    in a different profile that KalCode threads don't use, so there is nothing to run by hand. The
    provider keeps the credentials in that account's profile and KalCode never sees them. Gemini CLI
    keeps its sign-in in its own encrypted credential store, never in plaintext. Gemini derives that
    store's key from your computer and user names, so it keeps the sign-in out of plaintext but not
    away from other programs running as your user.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your usage of each provider is billed by that provider under your account, exactly as it is when
    you use it directly. KalCode does not charge for, resell or meter provider usage, holds no
    company provider credentials for your use, and has no KalCode-paid fallback. KalVoice does not
    use your provider accounts to interpret commands: interpretation runs on your computer, and
    there is no provider fallback.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported CLI versions</h2>
  <p>
    Managed accounts run the provider CLI release lines KalCode has certified: Claude Code 2.1.282
    or a later 2.1 release, Codex CLI 0.155.1 or a later release in the 0.155 to 0.158 lines, and
    Gemini CLI 0.61. Pre-release builds aren't supported. With any other version, sign-in and
    threads stop with a message that names the supported versions.
  </p>

  <h2>Several accounts per provider</h2>
  <p>
    You can add more than one account for each provider, such as a personal and a work account.
    Providers, then Accounts, shows each account's active threads and the workspaces where it is
    the default. <strong>Set default</strong> chooses the account new threads use where a workspace
    has no default of its own, and
    <strong>Connect another account</strong> adds an account and starts its sign-in. Gemini CLI
    accounts show the Google email they are signed in with.
  </p>
  <p>
    In New thread, <strong>Remember these accounts for this workspace</strong> keeps the accounts
    you chose, and KalCode selects them the next time you start a thread in that workspace.
  </p>

  <h2>Switching a thread's account</h2>
  <p>
    Each thread stays on the account it started with, and never changes account on its own, even
    when you change the default. To move a thread to another signed-in account, use the account
    menu in its header, the command palette (“switch gemini b”) or KalVoice (“Switch this Gemini
    thread to Gemini B”). KalCode asks you to confirm first. The thread's past conversation stays
    as it is; only future messages use the new account, starting a new provider session. An
    account that is signed out has to be signed in before a thread can move to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Detection</h2>
  <p>
    To find installed providers, KalCode looks for their executables on your <code>PATH</code> and in
    documented install locations, then runs only version and status commands, with a timeout. It
    reports whether each provider is installed, its version, whether it is signed in where the CLI
    exposes that, and what it can do. Detection never changes your machine; installing a provider
    only happens when you ask for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Isolation</h2>
  <p>
    Each provider session runs in its own supervised child process with a sanitized environment. If
    one provider fails, the others — and your terminals, workspaces and Dashboard — keep running.
  </p>

  <h2>One model for every provider</h2>
  <p>
    Provider-specific details are translated at the adapter boundary into KalCode's own concepts:
    normalized events, statuses, approval requests and capabilities. That is what lets the Dashboard,
    the permission model and KalVoice treat every provider the same way.
  </p>
</Docs>
